## VEX Robotics

During middle school, I was part of our STEAM club's VEX Robotics team. Over two
seasons, I led the robot's software development and contributed heavily to its
mechanical design and assembly.

## VEX 2022-2023: Spin Up

This marked our school's debut in VEX Robotics, coinciding with the first-ever
official national tournament hosted in Belgium.

The game objective was to launch yellow foam discs into an elevated basket, spin
field rollers to match your alliance's color, and trigger an endgame mechanism
to touch as many field tiles as possible for bonus points.

### Building the Robot

I took the lead on mechanical design. Our setup was unconventional and a bit
scrappy, but it proved remarkably effective. For the endgame, we engineered a
string launcher capable of expanding across nearly the entire field in seconds.

<AssetImage name="robot-2023.jpg" alt="Spin Up Robot" width="500" />

### Programming the Robot

For our first season, I programmed the robot using block-based coding (VEXcode
Blocks). While straightforward initially, the visual workspace eventually became
sluggish and cumbersome to navigate on my laptop as the codebase grew in
complexity.

### National Championship

We entered the Belgian national competition unsure of what to expect from other
teams. To our excitement, our alliance went on to win the tournament—securing
our ticket to the VEX World Championship in Dallas, Texas!

<AssetImage name="awards-2023.jpg" alt="National Competition Awards" width="500" />

### World Finals in Dallas

Traveling to Dallas to compete on the world stage in our very first season was
an incredible experience. While we didn't rank among the top global contenders,
competing against elite international teams was an eye-opening masterclass in
robotics engineering.

<div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4">
    <AssetImage name="block-code.jpg" caption="Programming in Blocks" />
    <AssetImage name="worlds-stand.jpg" caption="Our stand at the World Finals" />
</div>

## VEX 2023-2024: Over Under

The following season introduced a new challenge: scoring acorn-shaped "triballs"
into goals, climbing an elevated center pole during the endgame, and defending
against opponent descoring maneuvers.

Following the previous year's success, our robotics program expanded to include
a second team formed by younger students.

### Building "Mark E."

We designed our robot—nicknamed *Mark E.*—around an aggressive offensive
strategy. To slip underneath the goal barrier and rapidly descore triballs, we
had to engineer an ultra-low-profile chassis, which presented tight packaging
and mechanical constraints.

### Programming the robot in C++

Determined to level up my programming skills, I abandoned block code completely.
While I considered Python, my positive experience with low-level systems during
an internship at Philips pushed me straight toward modern C++.

### National Championship in Antwerp

The national tournament took place in Antwerp against significantly tougher
competition. On game day, I was asked to take the controls as the primary
driver. Despite the high-pressure matches, the robot handled reliably.

We advanced to the grand finals alongside our school's junior team and took the
title together! Because only one invitation to the Dallas World Finals was
awarded, our junior team earned the spot to represent Belgium.

<AssetImage name="robot-2024.webp" caption="Mark E. built for the Over Under season" width="500" />

To this day, our school has maintained a flawless 3-for-3 streak in national
championship titles.

## Codebase

The code for *Mark E.* is open source on
[GitHub](https://github.com/dotsem/over-under_mark-e). Looking back at it today,
it serves as a fun milestone showing just how much I've grown as a developer
since then.
